        description: "Assess the competitive landscape on the target trade lane. Identify all competing carriers operating direct and transshipment services on the route — for Middle East/Indian Subcontinent trade lanes, typical competitors include Maersk, MSC, CMA CGM, Hapag-Lloyd, ONE, Evergreen, HMM, Yang Ming, ZIM, and regional carriers (UASC legacy services via Hapag-Lloyd, IRISL, SCI). For each competitor, analyze: deployed vessel capacity (TEU per week), service frequency (weekly/fortnightly), transit time (port-to-port and door-to-door), port coverage (which ports they call at), reliability score (Sea-Intelligence Global Liner Performance), pricing positioning (premium/market/economy based on published tariffs and market intelligence), and alliance membership (2M, Ocean Alliance, THE Alliance — determines slot sharing). Identify competitive gaps: routes where competitors have poor reliability, ports with limited direct service, cargo types underserved (reefer, OOG), or customer segments neglected (SME shippers). Assess barriers to entry: terminal agreements, alliance slot commitments, cabotage restrictions (India coastal shipping), and regulatory requirements (port state requirements). Output competitive positioning map showing where the proposed service can differentiate.",
