        module: "Network Design", step: "Network design & route planning", type: "ai", processRef: "PRC-102", executorType: "ai_agent", phase: "Service Design", moduleUrl: "/fleet-deployment-planning/network-designs",
        description: "Design the optimal network configuration for the approved service. Determine the port rotation — the ordered sequence of ports the vessel will call at on each round voyage. Key considerations: (1) Geographic efficiency — minimize ballast (empty) steaming between cargo ports, avoid backtracking; (2) Cargo flow alignment — sequence ports to maximize laden utilization in both directions (e.g., westbound: loaded containers from Asia/India to Middle East; eastbound: re-export cargo, empty repositioning, or reefer exports from Oman/Yemen); (3) Hub connectivity — include major transshipment hubs (Jebel Ali, Salalah, Colombo, Singapore, Port Klang) to extend network reach beyond direct calls; (4) Port restrictions — check draft limitations (Nhava Sheva: 14m max, Mundra: 17m, Jebel Ali: 17.5m), air draft (bridges), LOA limits, and tidal windows; (5) Canal transit — if route involves Suez Canal, optimize northbound/southbound convoy timing (two convoys per day, transit time 12-16 hours); (6) Feeder connectivity — identify feeder ports that can connect to the mainline via hub ports. Model 3-5 rotation alternatives and rank by: total round voyage days, TEU-mile cost efficiency, port coverage vs demand map overlap, and competitive differentiation. Output: recommended port rotation with estimated voyage duration and vessel requirement.",
