        description: "Negotiate berthing windows with terminal operators at each port on the rotation. A berthing window defines the guaranteed arrival window (e.g., Tuesday 06:00-18:00) and estimated departure time for each weekly port call. Terminal operators allocate berths based on: vessel size (larger vessels get priority berths with deeper draft), expected move count (more container moves = more quay crane hours = longer berth occupation), shipping line volume commitment (major customers get preferred windows), and terminal capacity constraints (peak days are Monday-Wednesday at most container terminals). Key negotiation points: preferred day of week (align with customer supply chain schedules — e.g., Friday departure from Jebel Ali for Sunday arrival in Nhava Sheva matches Indian importers' Monday customs clearance), berth guarantee (priority berthing vs best-effort — priority costs more but avoids waiting at anchorage), quay crane allocation (minimum cranes per call, e.g., 3-4 gantry cranes for 4,000+ TEU vessel), productivity guarantee (moves per hour, typically 25-30 per crane for gantry cranes), and free time (days of free storage before demurrage charges, typically 3-7 days for import, 5-10 days for export). Negotiate at all ports simultaneously — changing one port's window affects the entire schedule cascade. Terminal agreements typically run 12 months, aligned with schedule publication period.",
