        description: "Generate the 52-week proforma schedule — the master timetable that defines every vessel's position for the next year. For each sailing (voyage number), calculate ETA (Estimated Time of Arrival) and ETD (Estimated Time of Departure) at every port on the rotation, factoring in: sea time between ports (distance ÷ speed, adjusted for weather routing — add 5-10% for Indian Ocean monsoon season Jun-Sep, 3-5% for Mediterranean winter), port stay duration (from terminal negotiations in step 15 — typically 12-36 hours depending on move count), canal transit time (Suez: 12-16 hours including convoy waiting; Panama: 8-10 hours), schedule buffer (2-4 hours per port call to absorb minor delays without cascade effect), and timezone changes along the route. Generate voyage numbers following convention: {service_code}{year}{sequence} (e.g., AGX2601, AGX2602... for Arabian Gulf Express 2026 voyage 1, 2, etc.). Assign specific vessels to voyage numbers based on the deployment plan — each vessel follows the same rotation but offset by the frequency interval. Ensure schedule feasibility: no vessel is required to exceed eco-speed on any leg (fuel cost explodes above eco-speed), adequate crew rest periods between ports (STCW requirements), and bunker range sufficient between bunkering ports (typically bunker every 2-3 port calls). The proforma is the promise to the market — once published, customers book against these ETAs.",
