        description: "Final approval gate before the schedule is published to the market. This is the most consequential gate in the entire flow — once published, the schedule becomes a binding commitment to customers, partners, terminals, and regulators. Decision-makers review: the complete 52-week proforma, schedule quality validation results (transit time competitiveness, feasibility, disruption resilience), fleet financial assessment (cost per voyage, break-even load factor), speed/fuel analysis (CII compliance, bunker budget), canal transit plan (if applicable), and berth window confirmations. Approval criteria: (1) Schedule Quality Score ≥ 75; (2) Transit times competitive on key port pairs; (3) No unresolved feasibility issues; (4) Bunker budget aligned with P&L projection; (5) All terminal agreements signed; (6) First sailing date achievable with current vessel positioning. If approved: schedule status changes to 'approved for publication', commercial teams begin rate filing, EDI schedules are prepared for transmission to customers and portals (INTTRA, CargoSmart), and marketing materials are prepared. The publication itself happens in step 21 (system automated). If rejected: specific issues must be resolved (e.g., adjust rotation, renegotiate terminal windows, change vessel assignment) and schedule resubmitted.",
