        description: "Initiate a formal incident investigation upon receiving a cargo damage report, vessel incident notification, or third-party claim. Gather initial facts: (1) Incident type — cargo damage (wet damage, contamination, shortage, breakage, theft, temperature deviation for reefer), vessel incident (collision, grounding, fire, structural failure), or third-party property damage; (2) Date, time, and location of incident (port, at sea, terminal); (3) Vessel name, voyage number, and BL references; (4) Initial damage description and estimated value; (5) Witnesses and crew statements; (6) Photographic/video evidence; (7) Weather and sea state conditions (from noon reports); (8) Container numbers and seal status. Create the incident record with a unique reference. Assess preliminary cause: carrier negligence (poor stowage, ventilation failure, reefer malfunction), inherent vice (cargo's own nature), force majeure (Act of God, war), or third-party fault (stevedore damage, terminal negligence). Flag for P&I Club early notification if estimated value exceeds USD 25,000.",
